    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Peloton does not really sell treadmills. It sells a membership, and the treadmill is the
          delivery mechanism — which is why the most important number in this decision is not the
          motor rating or the deck length, but the one that leaves your account every month for as
          long as you own the machine.
        </p>

        <div id="what-peloton-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Peloton Actually Sells</h2>
<p>
            There are two machines. The <strong>Tread</strong> is the smaller and far more common
            one — a conventional belt-over-deck treadmill with a motorised incline, a large
            touchscreen and two knurled control knobs, one for speed and one for incline, sitting
            where your hands naturally fall. The <strong>Tread+</strong> is the larger, much more
            expensive machine, and it is a fundamentally different device: it uses a slat belt
            rather than a continuous rubber belt, which changes how it feels, what it costs and
            what it needs from you in maintenance.
          </p>
          <p>
            The knobs are worth dwelling on, because they are the one piece of Peloton hardware
            design that is genuinely better than the competition rather than merely more expensive.
            Changing speed on most treadmills means finding a small button and pressing it
            repeatedly, or stabbing at a touchscreen while moving. On the Tread you roll a dial
            with your thumb without breaking stride or looking down. For interval work, where you
            might change speed thirty times in a session, that is not a luxury feature — it is the
            difference between doing the session as written and approximating it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Everything else about the machine is ordinary. The frame is solid, the deck is
            cushioned, the incline motor works, the belt is a belt. If you stripped the screen off
            and sold the Tread as a dumb treadmill, it would be a competent mid-to-upper-range
            machine at a price that would look high. That is the honest starting point, and every
            argument for or against Peloton proceeds from it.
          </p>
          <p>
            What you are paying the premium for is the software, the class library, the instructors
            and the sense that someone is expecting you. Whether that is worth it is a real
            question with a real answer for each person, and it is not a question about treadmills
            at all. It is a question about how you behave when nobody is watching.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscription" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Subscription Is the Product</h2>
<p>
            Peloton’s All-Access Membership is what turns the screen into the thing you saw in the
            advertisement. Without it the hardware still runs — you can walk, jog and set an
            incline using the knobs and a basic display — but the classes, the leaderboard, the
            programmes, the metrics history and effectively the entire reason you chose Peloton
            over a cheaper machine are behind it.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is not a criticism. It is a business model, it is clearly disclosed, and Peloton
            has never pretended otherwise. But it needs to be in the total, because a treadmill is
            a five-to-ten-year purchase and a membership is a five-to-ten-year commitment attached
            to it. Take the hardware price, add sixty months of membership, and compare that number
            — not the sticker price — against the alternatives. For most buyers the membership
            component over five years is comparable to the machine itself, and over ten years it
            is comfortably larger.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two details change the arithmetic in Peloton’s favour, and both are frequently missed.
            The first is that All-Access covers the household rather than the person, so a couple
            or a family splits that cost across several users, which materially improves the
            comparison against per-person gym memberships. The second is that the same membership
            covers Peloton’s other equipment and its app on phones, tablets and televisions, so if
            there is already a Peloton bike in the house, the marginal cost of adding a Tread is
            just the hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            One detail moves the other way. The membership is not optional in any practical sense,
            it is not locked at today’s price, and it does not stop when you stop. If you pause
            using the machine for six months, the choice is to keep paying or to lose access to the
            history and the programmes you have built up. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">no-subscription roundup</a>
            works through what that looks like across five years against machines that ask nothing
            after the purchase.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tread-vs-tread-plus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Tread vs Tread+: The Real Differences</h2>
<p>
            The gap between these two machines is much wider than the shared name suggests, and
            almost nobody should be genuinely torn between them — the correct choice is usually
            obvious once the differences are stated plainly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt.</strong> The Tread uses a conventional continuous belt running over a
            cushioned deck. The Tread+ uses a slat belt: dozens of individual slats on a bearing
            system, the same basic architecture used by high-end commercial machines. A slat belt
            feels firmer and more consistent underfoot, does not develop the dead spots a worn
            deck develops, and does not need lubricating. It is also heavier, far more expensive
            to build, and much more expensive to repair if something goes wrong outside warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Size and weight.</strong> The Tread+ is longer, wider and substantially
            heavier. Neither machine folds — this matters more than buyers expect, because the
            footprint you measure is the footprint you live with permanently. The Tread+ in
            particular is a piece of furniture you are committing a room to, and moving it is a
            planned event rather than a decision you make on a Sunday.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running feel.</strong> For anyone running seriously, the Tread+ is the better
            machine and the difference is not subtle. The deck is longer, which matters for stride
            at speed, and the slat surface stays consistent for years. For walkers and casual
            joggers, the Tread+ advantage is close to irrelevant and the price difference is very
            relevant indeed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The decision.</strong> Buy the Tread unless you are a genuine runner with the
            budget and the floor space, in which case the Tread+ is defensible and you should
            still cross-shop it against
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial-grade machines</a>
            at similar money, several of which offer comparable hardware without a monthly fee
            attached.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-history" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Recall History, Stated Plainly</h2>
<p>
            Any honest Peloton treadmill guide has to cover this, and a lot of them skip it.
          </p>
          <p>
            In 2021 the US Consumer Product Safety Commission announced recalls of both machines.
            The Tread+ recall followed reports of children and a pet being pulled under the rear of
            the machine, including one child fatality, and Peloton halted sales of the Tread+ for
            an extended period. The smaller Tread was recalled in the same period for a separate
            problem — touchscreens that could loosen and detach. Both are matters of public record
            rather than interpretation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Peloton subsequently reworked the Tread+ and returned it to sale with added safety
            measures, including a rear guard and a passcode-based lock that prevents the belt
            starting without a code being entered. The Tread’s screen hardware was addressed under
            its own recall remedy.
          </p>
          <p>
            What to take from this as a buyer in 2026: the machines on sale now are not the
            machines that were recalled, and the specific failure modes have been addressed. But
            the underlying physics has not changed and never will. A slat belt at the rear of a
            powerful treadmill is a genuine hazard to a small child or an animal, on any brand, and
            the rear clearance behind any treadmill is a safety zone rather than spare floor. If
            there are toddlers or pets in the house, the lock code is not an optional convenience
            feature, and the room the machine lives in should be one that closes.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is also the strongest argument for buying new rather than secondhand here. A used
            Tread+ of uncertain vintage may or may not have had its recall remedy applied, and
            establishing that from a private seller is difficult. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used buying checklist</a>
            covers the general case, but on this specific machine the safer answer is to buy
            through Peloton.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hardware-underneath" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What the Hardware Is Like Underneath the Screen</h2>
<p>
            Strip away the software and assess the Tread the way you would assess any treadmill,
            because in eight years that is exactly what it will be.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck length</strong> is the specification that decides whether a treadmill
            suits a runner, and the Tread is adequate rather than generous. It is long enough for
            most people to run on comfortably and short enough that a tall runner at speed should
            measure carefully first — our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tall runner guide</a>
            explains the stride arithmetic. The Tread+ is longer and this is one of the clearest
            justifications for its price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor</strong> is sufficient. Peloton does not compete on horsepower
            claims, which is to its credit given how meaningless peak ratings are, and the machine
            does not labour in normal use. If you are comparing specifications across brands, our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why continuous-duty ratings are the only ones worth comparing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Build quality</strong> is genuinely good. The frames are stable, the finish is
            better than the mid-market, and the machines do not develop the rattles that afflict
            cheaper treadmills in year two.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Serviceability</strong> is the weak point, and it is the one that should worry
            a long-term owner. This is a closed system with proprietary parts and a screen that is
            integral to the experience. A ten-year-old Sole or Horizon can be kept running with
            generic belts, standard rollers and a universal motor controller. A ten-year-old
            Peloton with a dead screen is a treadmill that has lost most of what you paid for,
            and the repair route runs through one company. That is a real risk on a purchase this
            size, and it is the single strongest structural argument against the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Who Peloton Is Genuinely Right For</h2>
<p>
            There is a real answer here, and it is neither the marketing answer nor the cynical
            one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Peloton is right for you if you do not train without being told to.</strong>
            This is the whole case and it is a strong one. A large proportion of home treadmills
            are used enthusiastically for six weeks and then become clothes racks, and the money
            wasted on an unused $1,200 treadmill is worse than the money spent on a more expensive
            machine you actually use. If structured classes and an instructor expecting you is the
            thing that gets you on the belt three times a week, Peloton is not overpriced. It is
            the cheapest thing in the house.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It is right if there is more than one user.</strong> Household coverage changes
            the per-person arithmetic substantially, and a family of three each doing two sessions
            a week is getting genuine value from a single membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It is right if you already own Peloton equipment.</strong> The marginal cost is
            hardware only, and the ecosystem argument is real rather than rhetorical.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It is wrong if you already train consistently on your own.</strong> If you run
            to a plan, follow your own intervals, or simply put a podcast on and go, you are paying
            a large recurring fee for a feature you will stop opening within a month. Buy better
            hardware instead — at Peloton money you can have a machine with a longer deck, a bigger
            motor and a warranty that does not expire, from
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> or
            <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness</a>, and never
            pay another penny.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It is wrong if the budget is tight.</strong> A stretched buyer who commits to a
            monthly fee alongside a finance agreement has built a fragile arrangement. Treadmills
            should not have ongoing obligations attached when money is a concern.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What to Buy Instead, and When</h2>
<p>
            If the classes are the appeal but the price is not, there is a well-established
            workaround that a lot of people end up at: buy good dumb hardware and run the Peloton
            app on a tablet propped in front of it. The app tier without the equipment is
            substantially cheaper than All-Access, the tread classes are the same classes, and what
            you lose is the auto-following of speed and incline and the leaderboard integration.
            You change the speed yourself when the instructor says to. For a lot of people that is
            a trivial loss for a large saving, and it decouples the hardware decision from the
            software one entirely — you can leave the app at any time and still own a good
            treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want comparable hardware without any of it, the cross-shop list is short and
            consistent. <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> builds
            heavy, plain machines with long warranties and no account requirement, and is the most
            common destination for people who looked at Peloton and decided against the
            membership. <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack</a>
            is the closest direct competitor in philosophy, with iFit playing the same role as
            All-Access — so it solves the hardware question and not the recurring-cost one.
            <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon</a> is the value
            answer, particularly for interval work, because its quick-dial controls address the
            same problem Peloton’s knobs address at a fraction of the price.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if the honest answer is that you mostly walk, the entire premium is misdirected.
            A walker is best served by
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">a machine built for walking</a>
            at a third of the money, because none of what Peloton is charging for — the class
            library, the performance hardware, the leaderboard — improves a walk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="five-year-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Running the Five-Year Number Yourself</h2>
<p>
            Do this before you order. It takes two minutes and it is the only comparison that
            reflects what you will actually pay.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One.</strong> Write down the hardware price including delivery, assembly and
            tax. Peloton delivery is generally handled properly, which has real value against a
            kerbside drop, but it is not free and it belongs in the total.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two.</strong> Multiply the current monthly membership by sixty. Use the price
            today and then add a margin for increases, because subscription pricing has moved
            upward across the industry and assuming five flat years is optimistic.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Three.</strong> Add the two together. That is the Peloton number.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Four.</strong> Do the same for the alternative: hardware price, delivery, and
            then the honest running costs, which for a conventional treadmill means lubricant
            every few months and a belt somewhere around year five. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a>
            has the real figures, and they are small — this is tens of dollars a year, not
            hundreds. Add
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">electricity</a>
            to both sides if you want to be thorough; it is similar for both and rarely changes the
            answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Five.</strong> Divide each total by the number of people who will genuinely use
            it, and by the number of sessions you honestly expect per week rather than the number
            you intend. A Peloton used four times a week by two people is excellent value. The same
            machine used twice a month by one person is the most expensive clothes rack available.
          </p>
          <p>
            The arithmetic does not produce a universal winner, which is exactly why it is worth
            doing. It produces your answer, and for a meaningful minority of buyers that answer is
            Peloton.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need a subscription for a Peloton treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not to make the belt move — the machine runs, inclines and displays basic metrics without one. But the classes, programmes, leaderboard and metrics history are all behind the All-Access Membership, and those are the reason to buy a Peloton rather than a cheaper treadmill. In practical terms the subscription is not optional, and it should be treated as part of the purchase price rather than an extra.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Peloton Tread and Tread+?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Tread is a conventional treadmill with a belt over a cushioned deck. The Tread+ uses a slat belt on bearings, is longer, heavier and considerably more expensive, and needs no lubrication. The Tread+ is the better machine for genuine running; for walking and casual jogging the difference is small and the price difference is large. Neither folds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Was the Peloton treadmill recalled?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. In 2021 the US Consumer Product Safety Commission announced recalls of both machines — the Tread+ after reports of people and pets being pulled under the rear, including one child fatality, and the Tread for touchscreens that could loosen and detach. Peloton later reworked the Tread+ with a rear guard and a passcode lock and returned it to sale. Current machines are not the recalled versions, but rear clearance and the lock code still matter in any household with children or pets.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Peloton treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it if the classes are what get you training and if more than one person in the household will use it, because the membership covers everyone. It is poor value if you already train consistently on your own — at the same total cost you can buy a machine with a longer deck, a larger motor and a lifetime frame warranty, and never pay a monthly fee.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use the Peloton app without buying the Tread?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the most common way to get most of the value for much less. A cheaper app-only tier runs on a phone or tablet propped in front of any treadmill, and the tread classes are the same classes. What you lose is automatic speed and incline following and leaderboard integration — you change the speed yourself when told. That also decouples the software from the hardware, so leaving the app later costs you nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Peloton Tread fold up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Neither the Tread nor the Tread+ folds, so the footprint you measure is the footprint you commit to permanently. This catches out buyers cross-shopping against folding machines, and it matters most in a shared room. Measure the unfolded length plus the run-off clearance behind the deck before ordering, and plan the delivery route as well as the destination.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Peloton Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Almost every regretted Peloton purchase traces to one of these four, and all four are avoidable before the order is placed rather than after.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing the sticker price against a rival’s sticker price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The hardware price is roughly half the story on a five-year view. Comparing a Peloton against a subscription-free machine on sticker alone understates the gap by an amount similar to the price of a second treadmill. Add sixty months of membership to one side before you compare, and add a margin for price increases while you are there.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it to create motivation you do not have yet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The classes work well for people who respond to being coached and do very little for people who do not. Nobody knows which they are from the advertisement. If you have owned exercise equipment before, your own history with it is far better evidence than any review — including this one. Try the app on a phone for a month first; it costs a fraction and answers the question honestly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring that it does not fold, and that it is heavy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Neither machine folds and both are heavy, so the space commitment is permanent and relocating one is a planned event. Measure the unfolded footprint plus run-off clearance behind the deck, check the delivery route including stairwell turns and door widths, and decide whether the room works with a treadmill in it forever, not just this month.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating rear clearance as spare floor space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The area behind any treadmill is where you land if you lose your footing, and on a slat-belt machine it is also the specific hazard that led to the 2021 Tread+ recall. Keep it clear, use the passcode lock every time if there are children or pets in the house, and put the machine in a room that can be closed rather than an open living space.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

