        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Peloton builds good hardware and sells it at a price that only makes sense if you use the
            software. <strong class="text-white">Add sixty months of membership to the sticker
            price before you compare it with anything</strong> — that is the real number, and it is
            roughly double what the product page implies.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If structured classes are what get you training, it is defensible and possibly cheap. If
            you already train on your own, buy
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">plainer hardware</a>
            with a longer warranty and put the difference nowhere. And if you mostly walk, the
            entire premium is aimed at something you are not doing —
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">a walking machine</a>
            does that job for a third of the money.
          </p>
